{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The electron transport chain (respiratory chain) is a set of protein complexes and carriers in the inner mitochondrial membrane. It passes electrons from NADH and FADH2 to oxygen, forming water. The energy released pumps protons across the membrane, and the flow of protons back through ATP synthase makes ATP. This coupling is called oxidative phosphorylation.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## Where the chain fits

Cells break down carbohydrates, fats and amino acids in stages. First, complex molecules are split into building blocks. Next, these are degraded to acetyl CoA and a few other small molecules, which captures only a little energy as ATP. In the third stage, acetyl CoA is oxidised in the citric acid (Krebs) cycle, which is the final common pathway for the oxidation of all foodstuffs. Most of the energy at this stage is trapped in the reduced coenzymes **NADH** and **FADH2**.

{% figure src="/learn-images/biochemistry/electron-transport-chain/etc-overview.webp" alt="Diagram of the electron transport chain complexes in the inner mitochondrial membrane." caption="Electron flow through the complexes drives proton pumping and ATP synthesis." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Electron_transport_chain.svg" width="960" height="509" /%}

The **electron transport chain (ETC)**, also called the respiratory chain, is the final stage. It reoxidises NADH and FADH2 and passes their electrons to oxygen, which is reduced to water. The largest share of the oxygen the body uses is consumed here. ATP, the energy currency of the cell, links energy-releasing (catabolic) reactions to energy-requiring (anabolic) reactions.

## Where it sits: mitochondrial organisation

Mitochondria have a double membrane.

{% figure src="/learn-images/biochemistry/electron-transport-chain/mitochondrion-structure.webp" alt="Labelled diagram of a mitochondrion." caption="The inner membrane folds into cristae and holds the respiratory chain." credit="Kelvinsong; modified by Sowlos, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitochondrion_structure.svg" width="960" height="672" /%}

- **Inner mitochondrial membrane:** carries the ETC and the ATP-making system. It is rich in protein and is **impermeable to ions** (H+, K+, Na+) and to small molecules such as ADP and ATP. It is highly folded into **cristae**, which greatly increase its surface area. Its inner surface carries lollipop-shaped particles, the phosphorylating subunits, where ATP is made.
- **Matrix:** the interior. It is rich in the enzymes of the citric acid cycle, fatty acid beta-oxidation and amino acid oxidation.

Mitochondria are present in all cells except mature erythrocytes.

## The five complexes and mobile carriers

The inner membrane can be separated into five complexes. Complexes I to IV carry electrons. Complex V makes ATP. Besides the complexes there are mobile carriers: **NADH, coenzyme Q, cytochrome c and oxygen**.

{% table %}
- **Complex**
- **Name**
- **What it does**
---
- **I**
- NADH-coenzyme Q reductase (NADH dehydrogenase)
- Takes electrons from NADH using bound FMN and iron-sulfur centres, and passes them to coenzyme Q
---
- **II**
- Succinate-coenzyme Q reductase (succinate dehydrogenase)
- Takes electrons from succinate through FAD (giving FADH2) and iron-sulfur centres to coenzyme Q
---
- **III**
- Coenzyme QH2-cytochrome c reductase (cytochrome bc1)
- Passes electrons from reduced coenzyme Q to cytochrome c
---
- **IV**
- Cytochrome c oxidase (cytochromes a and a3)
- Passes electrons to oxygen and forms water
---
- **V**
- ATP synthase
- Makes ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate (Pi)
{% /table %}

Each complex is made of 4 to 35 individual proteins. Complex II is encoded only by nuclear genes. The others are encoded by nuclear or mitochondrial DNA, and mitochondrial DNA is inherited only from the mother.

## Components in the order of electron flow

**1. Nicotinamide nucleotides (NAD+).** Dehydrogenases remove two hydrogen atoms from a substrate. Both electrons but only one proton (a hydride ion) go to NAD+, forming NADH plus a free proton. Substrates include glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, pyruvate, isocitrate, alpha-ketoglutarate and malate. NADPH is not usually a substrate for the ETC. It is used mainly for anabolic reactions such as fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis.

**2. NADH dehydrogenase (Complex I).** A flavoprotein with **FMN** as its prosthetic group. FMN accepts two electrons and two protons to become FMNH2. The complex is closely associated with iron-sulfur proteins, and these centres are needed to pass the hydrogen atoms on to coenzyme Q.

**3. Succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II).** Also a flavoprotein, with **FAD** as its coenzyme. It sits in the inner membrane and accepts two hydrogen atoms from succinate: succinate + FAD gives fumarate + FADH2. Electrons from fatty acid oxidation (through acyl CoA dehydrogenase and the electron-transferring flavoprotein) also enter at the coenzyme Q level, so they bypass Complex I.

**4. Iron-sulfur (FeS) proteins.** They exist in the oxidised (Fe3+) or reduced (Fe2+) state. One FeS protein helps move electrons from FMN to coenzyme Q, and others are linked with cytochromes b and c1. About half a dozen have been identified in the respiratory chain. Their exact mechanism is not clearly understood.

**5. Coenzyme Q (ubiquinone).** A quinone derivative with a long, hydrophobic isoprenoid tail. Mammalian tissues have 10 isoprenoid units, so it is CoQ10. It is **lipophilic and mobile**. It accepts electrons from FMNH2 (Complex I) and from FADH2 formed outside the ETC (succinate dehydrogenase, acyl CoA dehydrogenase), and passes them to Complex III. In this way it links the flavoproteins to the cytochromes. It is the only member of the chain that is not a protein. It has no known vitamin precursor in animals and is synthesised in the body.

**6. Cytochromes.** Each has a **heme** group (a porphyrin ring plus iron). Unlike the iron in hemoglobin, cytochrome iron is reversibly changed between Fe3+ and Fe2+ as part of its job as an electron carrier. Electrons pass from coenzyme Q to cytochromes b and c1 (Complex III), then cytochrome c, then cytochromes a and a3 (Complex IV). Cytochrome c is on the outer face of the inner membrane and, like coenzyme Q, is a mobile carrier.

**7. Cytochrome a + a3 (cytochrome oxidase).** This is the only carrier whose heme iron has a free coordination site that can react directly with molecular oxygen. Here the electrons, oxygen and protons come together and oxygen is reduced to **water**. The complex also contains **copper**.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Order of the chain:** NADH, then FMN (Complex I), then CoQ, then cytochrome b and c1 (Complex III), then cytochrome c, then cytochrome a and a3 (Complex IV), then oxygen. Succinate joins at CoQ through Complex II. Remember: the components are arranged in order of increasingly positive redox potential.
{% /callout %}

## Why electrons flow: redox potential

Oxidation (loss of electrons) of one compound always goes with reduction (gain of electrons) of another. NAD+ and NADH form a **redox pair**, as do FMN and FMNH2. Each pair has a standard reduction potential, E0, measured in volts.

- The more negative the E0, the greater the tendency of the reduced member to **give up** electrons.
- The more positive the E0, the greater the tendency of the oxidised member to **accept** electrons.
- Electrons flow from the pair with the more negative E0 to the pair with the more positive E0.

The NAD+/NADH pair has an E0 of about -0.32 V and the oxygen/water pair about +0.82 V. The difference is **1.14 V**, which is equal to about 52 Cal/mol of free energy. The change in free energy is directly related to the change in E0. Free energy is therefore released step by step as electrons pass down the chain.

The electrons are carried in different forms: as hydride ions to NAD+, as hydrogen atoms to FMN, coenzyme Q and FAD, and as electrons alone to the cytochromes.

## How the energy makes ATP: chemiosmotic hypothesis

Two explanations were proposed for oxidative phosphorylation.

{% figure src="/learn-images/biochemistry/electron-transport-chain/chemiosmosis-atp-synthase.webp" alt="Diagram of ATP synthase using the proton gradient to make ATP." caption="ATP synthase turns the proton gradient into ATP." credit="Chiswick Chap, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chemiosmotic_coupling_mitochondrion.svg" width="960" height="540" /%}

- **Chemical coupling hypothesis:** high-energy phosphorylated intermediates form during electron transfer and are used to make ATP, like substrate-level phosphorylation. It lacks experimental evidence, because no such intermediate has ever been isolated.
- **Chemiosmotic hypothesis:** the one now widely accepted. It compares the system to a battery, with energy stored by separation of positive and negative charges.

In the chemiosmotic hypothesis, the inner mitochondrial membrane is impermeable to protons. As electrons pass along the chain, protons are pumped across the membrane. This builds a **proton gradient**, and the resulting proton motive force drives ATP synthesis. Protons flow back through **ATP synthase (Complex V)**, which works as a rotary motor to make ATP from ADP and Pi.

Oxidation of the reducing equivalents and ATP synthesis are tightly coupled in most circumstances: one cannot go on unless the other is working.

## P:O ratio and ATP yield

The **P:O ratio** is the number of ATP made per atom of oxygen reduced (per pair of electrons passed along the chain).

{% table %}
- **Coenzyme**
- **Classical P:O ratio**
- **Recent evidence**
- **Protons pumped**
---
- **NADH**
- 3
- 2.5
- 10
---
- **FADH2**
- 2
- 1.5
- 6
{% /table %}

Synthesis of one ATP needs four protons, which explains the recent values. Classically, three sites in the ETC release enough energy to make ATP, called coupling sites:

1. Oxidation of FMNH2 by coenzyme Q
2. Oxidation of cytochrome b by cytochrome c1
3. The cytochrome oxidase reaction

FADH2 enters at coenzyme Q, so the first site is bypassed and only two coupling sites remain. This is why FADH2 gives fewer ATP than NADH.

In the citric acid cycle, oxidation of 3 NADH through the ETC gives 9 ATP and FADH2 gives 2 ATP. Together with one ATP from substrate-level phosphorylation, one acetyl CoA yields 12 ATP (10 ATP according to recent evidence).

**Efficiency:** when NADH is oxidised, the classical 3 ATP trap about 21.9 Cal (each ATP is 7.3 Cal), roughly **42% of the energy**. The rest is lost as heat. This heat is not wasted: it keeps the chain running and helps maintain body temperature.

## Inhibitors and uncouplers

Because the components are linked like meshed gears, blocking electron transfer at any one site stops electron flow from substrate to oxygen.

{% table %}
- **Site of block**
- **Examples**
- **Notes**
---
- **Complex I (between NADH and coenzyme Q)**
- Rotenone (fish poison), amytal, piericidin A
- Stops electrons leaving NADH
---
- **Between cytochrome b and c1 (Complex III)**
- Antimycin A
- Blocks the chain at the cytochrome bc1 step
---
- **Cytochrome oxidase (Complex IV)**
- Cyanide, carbon monoxide, sodium azide
- Cyanide binds the Fe3+ of cytochrome oxidase and is probably the most potent inhibitor
{% /table %}

**Cyanide poisoning:** cyanide blocks mitochondrial respiration and leads to cell death. Death is due to tissue asphyxia, mostly of the central nervous system.

**Uncouplers** such as 2,4-dinitrophenol separate electron transport from ATP synthesis. Oxidation continues but the energy is released as heat instead of making ATP. In brown adipose tissue, excess calories are burned and released as heat instead of being stored as fat, and active brown adipose tissue is believed to protect some people from becoming obese.

## Reactive oxygen species

If oxygen is only partly reduced, **reactive oxygen species (ROS)** form: superoxide, hydrogen peroxide and the hydroxyl radical. They damage DNA and proteins and cause lipid peroxidation. The cell defends itself with **superoxide dismutase, catalase and glutathione peroxidase**.

## Vitamins that keep energy metabolism running

Four B vitamins are essential for the citric acid cycle and hence energy generation:

- **Riboflavin (as FAD):** cofactor for succinate dehydrogenase. Its coenzyme forms, FMN and FAD, help transfer electrons in the ETC. Deficiency (ariboflavinosis) causes cheilosis and corneal vascularisation.
- **Niacin (as NAD+):** electron acceptor for isocitrate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and malate dehydrogenase.
- **Thiamine (as TPP):** coenzyme for alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.
- **Pantothenic acid (as coenzyme A):** carries acetyl and succinyl groups.

## Clinical correlation: mitochondrial disease

Mitochondria work as biological fuel cells, producing ATP by aerobic metabolism. **Primary mitochondrial disease** results from deficient respiratory chain function. The cause may be mutations in genes for respiratory chain subunits, assembly factors or cofactors, or in the genes that maintain mitochondrial DNA, whether in nuclear or in mitochondrial DNA.

- Defects of oxidative phosphorylation can affect any tissue, but the most **energy-dependent organs** are the most vulnerable: brain, heart, muscle, kidney, liver and others.
- Consider the diagnosis in a patient of any age with **progressive, multisystem** disease that no specific diagnosis explains.
- Defects in any of the complexes cause **chronic lactic acidosis**, presumably because the redox state changes and NADH rises. A lactate-to-pyruvate ratio of 25 or more is highly suggestive of respiratory chain dysfunction, although severe liver disease can also raise it. Some patients have normal or only mildly raised lactate even during a metabolic crisis.
- Skeletal muscle and heart are usually involved. Muscle biopsy under electron microscopy can show abnormal cristae.
- Examples include Leigh syndrome, Pearson syndrome, Kearns-Sayre syndrome and Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy.
- Common features in children include fatigue, exercise intolerance, weakness, gastrointestinal problems, ataxia and developmental delay.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Inhibitor sites:** "Rotenone at the Root (Complex I), Antimycin in the Middle (Complex III), Cyanide at the End (Complex IV)." Cyanide, carbon monoxide and azide all stop cytochrome oxidase.
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**FADH2 skips a site:** FADH2 enters at coenzyme Q, so it bypasses coupling site 1 and gives fewer ATP (P:O 2 classical, 1.5 recent) than NADH (3 classical, 2.5 recent).
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Cytochrome a and a3 (cytochrome oxidase) is the only carrier whose heme iron has a free coordination site that can react directly with molecular oxygen." %}
The component of the electron transport chain that reacts directly with molecular oxygen is:
- A. Coenzyme Q
- B. Cytochrome c
- C. Cytochrome a + a3
- D. NADH dehydrogenase
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="FADH2 enters the chain at coenzyme Q and bypasses the first coupling site, so only two coupling sites remain and the classical P:O ratio is 2." %}
The classical P:O ratio for the oxidation of FADH2 is:
- A. 1
- B. 2
- C. 3
- D. 4
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Cyanide binds the Fe3+ of cytochrome oxidase (Complex IV), blocking mitochondrial respiration. Rotenone acts at Complex I and antimycin A between cytochrome b and c1." %}
Cyanide inhibits the electron transport chain at:
- A. Complex I
- B. Between cytochrome b and c1
- C. Coenzyme Q
- D. Cytochrome oxidase
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Coenzyme Q is a quinone derivative with a long isoprenoid side chain (10 units in mammals, so CoQ10). Cytochromes and iron-sulfur proteins have no isoprenoid units." %}
Which component of the chain has isoprenoid units?
- A. Coenzyme Q
- B. Cytochrome c
- C. Cytochrome b
- D. Non-heme iron protein
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The inner mitochondrial membrane is impermeable to protons. This is what allows the pumped protons to build a gradient that drives ATP synthase." %}
Why is the impermeability of the inner mitochondrial membrane to H+ important?
- A. It lets NADH cross the membrane
- B. It lets ATP leave the matrix freely
- C. It allows a proton gradient to build up across the membrane
- D. It allows oxygen to diffuse into the matrix
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="In an uncoupler such as 2,4-dinitrophenol, oxidation continues but energy is released as heat instead of being used to make ATP." %}
The action of an uncoupler such as 2,4-dinitrophenol is to:
- A. Stop electron flow at cytochrome oxidase
- B. Separate electron transport from ATP synthesis, releasing energy as heat
- C. Block ATP synthase directly
- D. Increase the P:O ratio
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the electron transport chain and where is it located?" %}
A series of protein complexes and carriers in the inner mitochondrial membrane that passes electrons from NADH and FADH2 to oxygen, forming water. It is the final stage of oxidising reducing equivalents, and is coupled to ATP synthesis.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the five complexes." %}
I: NADH-coenzyme Q reductase. II: succinate-coenzyme Q reductase. III: coenzyme QH2-cytochrome c reductase. IV: cytochrome c oxidase. V: ATP synthase. Complexes I to IV carry electrons, V makes ATP.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which carriers are mobile?" %}
Coenzyme Q and cytochrome c, in addition to NADH and oxygen.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Explain the chemiosmotic hypothesis." %}
Electron flow pumps protons across the impermeable inner mitochondrial membrane, building a proton gradient and proton motive force. Protons flow back through ATP synthase and drive synthesis of ATP from ADP and Pi.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the P:O ratio?" %}
The number of ATP made per atom of oxygen reduced. Classically 3 for NADH and 2 for FADH2; recent evidence gives 2.5 and 1.5, based on 10 and 6 protons pumped and 4 protons per ATP.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why does FADH2 give less ATP than NADH?" %}
FADH2 enters at coenzyme Q, bypassing the first coupling site, so fewer protons are pumped.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How does mitochondrial disease present, and what is a useful biochemical clue?" %}
As progressive multisystem disease, worst in energy-dependent organs. Chronic lactic acidosis is common, and a lactate-to-pyruvate ratio of 25 or more is highly suggestive of respiratory chain dysfunction.
{% /viva %}

